WEBVTT

00:00.499 --> 00:04.560
Let's say that today-- and
we'll call today Day 1--

00:04.560 --> 00:08.000
Day 1 is a Monday.

00:08.000 --> 00:09.830
What I want to
figure out is what

00:09.830 --> 00:13.600
is the Day 300 going to be?

00:13.600 --> 00:15.857
What day of the week
will Day 300 be?

00:15.857 --> 00:17.440
And I encourage you
to pause the video

00:17.440 --> 00:19.800
and think about
that a little bit.

00:19.800 --> 00:22.100
So let's just write out
the days of the week.

00:22.100 --> 00:26.090
You have Monday-- I'll do this
in a different color-- you have

00:26.090 --> 00:37.140
Monday, Tuesday, Wednesday,
Thursday, Friday, Saturday

00:37.140 --> 00:39.226
and Sunday.

00:39.226 --> 00:40.600
So if we had a
lower number here,

00:40.600 --> 00:43.230
we could just fill this out.

00:43.230 --> 00:44.380
Monday is Day 1.

00:44.380 --> 00:45.490
Tuesday's Day 2.

00:45.490 --> 00:46.450
Wednesday, Day 3.

00:46.450 --> 00:48.794
4, 5, 6, 7.

00:48.794 --> 00:49.460
I'll keep going.

00:49.460 --> 00:52.030
Day 8, well, that's going
to be a Monday again.

00:52.030 --> 00:53.190
9 10.

00:53.190 --> 00:54.920
I'm almost writing
a calendar out.

00:54.920 --> 01:01.270
11, 12, 13, 14, 15, 16.

01:01.270 --> 01:02.510
So this is kind of useful.

01:02.510 --> 01:04.051
I could just write
it out if I wanted

01:04.051 --> 01:06.520
to figure out something
Day 16 or Day 20.

01:06.520 --> 01:08.220
I could just write that out.

01:08.220 --> 01:10.806
But this isn't that helpful if
I want to figure out Day 300

01:10.806 --> 01:12.180
or especially
wouldn't be helpful

01:12.180 --> 01:14.920
if I wanted to
figure out Day 3,000.

01:14.920 --> 01:17.360
So can I come up with
some mathematical way

01:17.360 --> 01:20.750
of thinking about what
Day 300 is going to be?

01:20.750 --> 01:24.130
Well, as you see when I
started drawing this grid here,

01:24.130 --> 01:25.540
this grid has rows.

01:25.540 --> 01:27.895
And each row, you
have seven days in it.

01:27.895 --> 01:28.770
And that makes sense.

01:28.770 --> 01:31.307
There are seven
days of the week.

01:31.307 --> 01:33.390
So is there a way that if
someone just gave you 16

01:33.390 --> 01:34.960
without drawing
this grid, then you

01:34.960 --> 01:38.060
would know that 16 is a Tuesday?

01:38.060 --> 01:40.800
Well, one way to think about
it that might jump out at you

01:40.800 --> 01:43.180
is you could divide 16 by 7.

01:43.180 --> 01:47.680
That will tell you how many of
these rows will come before 16.

01:52.510 --> 01:55.110
So 16 divided by 7 is 2.

01:55.110 --> 01:58.440
You have 2 rows before
16 right over here.

01:58.440 --> 02:01.340
You could get 7 into 16 2 times.

02:01.340 --> 02:04.720
And then you have a remainder.

02:04.720 --> 02:08.020
What is the remainder
when you divide 16 by 7?

02:08.020 --> 02:12.690
16 divided by 7
is going to be 2.

02:12.690 --> 02:14.400
2 times 7 is 14.

02:14.400 --> 02:16.610
You're going to have
a remainder of 2.

02:16.610 --> 02:18.870
So when we divide,
we've historically

02:18.870 --> 02:21.230
cared more about this 2.

02:21.230 --> 02:23.010
We normally care
more about well,

02:23.010 --> 02:24.430
how many times
does it go into it.

02:24.430 --> 02:27.590
But now, the remainder
is actually interesting.

02:27.590 --> 02:30.050
The remainder is really
interesting here.

02:30.050 --> 02:32.810
Because the remainder
tells you-- the first two

02:32.810 --> 02:35.200
just tells you 7
goes into 16 2 times.

02:35.200 --> 02:38.020
That's how many rows you have
before getting to the 16.

02:38.020 --> 02:42.410
But then the remainder tells
you in that row where is the 16?

02:42.410 --> 02:44.630
So the 16 is remainder 2.

02:44.630 --> 02:46.250
So the 16 is not the first.

02:46.250 --> 02:49.940
It's the second entry
in the third row.

02:49.940 --> 02:52.300
And so it's going
to be a Tuesday.

02:52.300 --> 02:54.257
Tuesday is the second day.

02:54.257 --> 02:55.340
I know what you're saying.

02:55.340 --> 02:56.430
Does that always work?

02:56.430 --> 02:59.090
Well, let's try it out
with some other examples.

02:59.090 --> 03:03.490
Let's imagine Day 25.

03:03.490 --> 03:06.240
So let's just divide 25 by 7.

03:06.240 --> 03:08.330
So I'll do it right
over-- I could do it.

03:08.330 --> 03:10.140
I'm going to make sure
I have enough space.

03:10.140 --> 03:15.940
So if I have 7 goes into
25, it goes 3 times.

03:15.940 --> 03:17.770
3 times 7 is 21.

03:17.770 --> 03:20.080
You have a remainder of 4.

03:20.080 --> 03:21.340
So let's see.

03:21.340 --> 03:25.240
So based on that-- so let
me rewrite this-- so 25

03:25.240 --> 03:29.785
divided by 7 is equal
to 3, remainder 4.

03:33.970 --> 03:36.340
So based on this, if we
were to write out the grid,

03:36.340 --> 03:39.740
we should have three rows of
7 before we get to the 25.

03:39.740 --> 03:43.680
And then 25 should sit
in the fourth column.

03:43.680 --> 03:46.310
So if it's sitting
in the fourth column,

03:46.310 --> 03:47.920
it should be a Thursday.

03:47.920 --> 03:52.120
So Day 25, based on this
little math we just did,

03:52.120 --> 03:53.300
should be a Thursday.

03:53.300 --> 03:55.810
Let's see if that
actually works out.

03:55.810 --> 04:06.086
So let's go to 17, 18, 19,
20, 21, 22, 23, 24 and 25.

04:06.086 --> 04:08.390
It is indeed a Thursday.

04:08.390 --> 04:09.970
But it makes complete sense.

04:09.970 --> 04:14.390
You could get seven rows in it
before the row that gets to 25.

04:14.390 --> 04:18.180
And then in that row, it's
going to be the fourth entry,

04:18.180 --> 04:19.940
because you have
a remainder of 4.

04:19.940 --> 04:21.360
1, 2, 3, 4.

04:21.360 --> 04:22.900
It's going to be a Thursday.

04:22.900 --> 04:24.950
So now we're ready to
answer the question.

04:24.950 --> 04:27.570
What is Day 300 going to be?

04:27.570 --> 04:34.560
So let's just divide 300
by 7 and see where we get.

04:34.560 --> 04:38.510
7 goes into 30 4 times.

04:38.510 --> 04:40.900
4 times 7 is 28.

04:40.900 --> 04:42.240
Subtract, you get 2.

04:42.240 --> 04:43.760
Bring down a 0.

04:43.760 --> 04:46.530
7 goes into 20 2 times.

04:46.530 --> 04:49.450
2 times 7 is 14.

04:49.450 --> 04:51.730
And then we get our remainder.

04:51.730 --> 04:53.860
And now we care much
more about the remainder.

04:53.860 --> 04:56.970
20 minus 14 is 6.

04:56.970 --> 04:59.870
So our remainder is 6.

04:59.870 --> 05:03.080
So if we think about what day
of the week it is, in its row

05:03.080 --> 05:04.684
it's going to be
the sixth entry.

05:04.684 --> 05:06.100
It's going to be
the sixth column.

05:06.100 --> 05:08.290
There's going to be
42 rows above it.

05:08.290 --> 05:11.180
But we care about which
entry it is in its row.

05:11.180 --> 05:15.510
So Day 300 is going to be
the sixth day of the week,

05:15.510 --> 05:16.810
the way we've written it out.

05:16.810 --> 05:21.398
It is going to be a Saturday.
